 that it  takes to get  up from mexico to like basically the  northeast  because the butterflies don't live that  long there's a lot of challenges along  the way  so the butterflies that you're seeing  now like in the fall in connecticut are  going to be  several gen they're gonna be like one or  two generations separate from the moths  that left mexico  in the spring and then basically when  there's to get these subtle cues from  the environment  they turn around and decide to return to  mexico which is  a mystery that science is still trying  to figure out how they do this but it's  like  that when it gets cooler my the monarchs  turn around and start flooding back  south usually along the coast and a lot  of people see a lot of monarchs around  this time of year because monarchs are  extremely um like they're very sensitive  to cold so they cannot survive winter  temperatures in the northeast  so they have to immediately return south  so over  multiple generations again um these a  lot of adult monarchs will actually move  from  say the northeast down to the  mid-atlantic or the southeast  um and then have another generation and  then those  next generation monarchs will continue  to mexico or the caribbean  so again a single monarch usually  doesn't make the whole trip there might  be a few that are strong enough to do it  but most of them  take a couple generations to make the  trip  and so given our  basically how we can affect  my by graders becomes very apparent when  i talk about these next two  species which are called this is the  common painted lady which if you have  children a lot of people  sell rearing kits for these where  children can rear  and rear butterflies from eggs to adult  and they're called painted ladies  they're very common species  and this butterfly here is called the  common snout  they're called snouts because they have  a little like their palps are  kind of pointed into a it looks like a  nose this is another migratory species  and sometimes in the southwest these  things are so  abundant that there could be literally  swarms of billions of them  flying through the southwest and people  have drove through them and  it just darkens the skies there's so  many of these  so when it comes to migrators  the common denominator in a lot of these  is the southwest  so in the southwestern united states you  have a lot of  concentration of butterfly diversity and  a lot of our  species that we get up into the  northeast of all different types  pass through that area and so a big  challenge  for going forward for humanity is to  actually protect  the areas not only in the in mexico in  latin america where these species  overwinter  but also protecting habitat that these  species use as corridors  to basically fuel up for their journey  so  old farmland old fields abandoned areas  with flowers are very important  for basically preserving the habitat  of these butterflies and moths and a lot  of these butterflies actually  feed on like these feed on milkweeds so  if you have milkweeds in your yard  don't don't chop them down keep them  because these butterflies  will use them both for fueling the next  generation and also food  for nectaring so and another issue  is associated with the border wall  that's unfortunately  being proposed to going up along the  texas  and new mexico and california border  and this wall is having the adverse  effect of potentially destroying lots of  key habitat for butterflies and moths  right along the texas border  and there is a very strong center of  diversity in southern texas it's one of  the most  species-rich areas of all the areas of  the united states for butterflies  and actually the the butter one of the  major butterfly gardens  in southern texas is actually partially  destroyed by construction crews that are  building the wall  so even by doing things that seem to be  based more focused on humans they have  adverse  impacts on the environment by causing  that destruction to the habitat both to  the plants that the  insects depend on and also killing  potential  caterpillars eggs and pupae of adult  butterflies and moths as well

